00:01:50.182 --> 00:01:52.140
NAOMI SEYFER: So this
talk is about serverless.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.140
What is even serverless?

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.950
It's a buzzword that you
may have been hearing.

00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:02.160
It's hot in the cloud
compute sphere these days.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.290
Our conception of it is
kind of a catch-all term.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:10.020
It has a few components that if
a compute product meets them,

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.820
we consider it under the
umbrella term of serverless.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:14.970
The first of those
is that you don't

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.273
have to worry about
infrastructure and management.

00:02:17.273 --> 00:02:18.690
You might hear me
repeat this "you

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:20.813
don't have to worry about"
phrase pretty often.

00:02:20.813 --> 00:02:22.230
"You don't have
to worry about" is

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:23.820
kind of the theme of serverless.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:26.370
So first, you don't have to
worry about infrastructure

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:27.390
management.

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:30.480
No VM provisioning,
no metal provisioning,

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.570
no bare metal provisioning.

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:36.040
Your scaling is going
to be automatic,

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:41.910
and it will scale up and
down as your needs require.

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:45.570
Another element of it is that
your security is fully managed.

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:49.530
You don't have to worry about
some kernel vulnerability

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:54.600
or, for a lot of the products,
some library vulnerability

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:55.750
happening.

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:57.870
All of those are updated
behind the scenes

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.990
so you don't have
to worry about it.

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.810
And you also don't have to
worry about paying for resources

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:05.670
that you're not using.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:07.905
The products that we
consider serverless,

00:03:07.905 --> 00:03:09.960
you pay only for what you use.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.570
You pay only for the CPU
and memory and requests

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.050
that you're using.

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:18.240
And when nobody is using the
service, it scales down to zero

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.240
and you don't pay
anything for that.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:25.080
So in this talk, we'll talk you
through the three serverless

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.960
compute options that
Google Cloud has--

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.000
Cloud Functions, App
Engine, and Cloud Run--

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:32.950
and when you might want
to use each of them,

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:37.550
because they each have their
strengths and weaknesses.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:40.690
These options lie along the
spectrum of serverless, which

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:44.440
is part of kind of a larger
spectrum of computation, where

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:48.790
at one end you just
write a tiny bit of code

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:51.010
and you send it to a server
and it runs it for you,

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:54.430
like Google Cloud Functions,
and on the other end of that you

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:58.850
have requisitioning blade
servers to go put in your data

00:03:58.850 --> 00:03:59.350
center.

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:02.290
But we won't talk about that
because that's not serverless.

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:06.340
So we'll talk about the spectrum
of serverless compute, where

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:10.840
at one end you have more ease
of use and at the other end

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:13.840
you have more control, though
they're all with good control

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.870
and good ease of use.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:20.352
So what will we build?

00:04:20.352 --> 00:04:22.310
MYLES BORINS: What we
ended up collaborating on

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:24.800
is a URL link
shortening service.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.830
It's built entirely
servicely on an architecture

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:29.220
that we'll show you momentarily.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.970
But perhaps the first thing
that we should show you

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:34.800
is the product itself.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:38.220
So I'm going to come over here
and get onto the demo machine

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:38.720
here.

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:45.950
And we're going to
go to min.kni.sh.

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:47.360
Small kni.sh.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.490
And we can see
the basic UI here.

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:52.760
This has been built
with Material for Web.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:54.000
We can click on the URL here.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.980
And let's make a
shortlink for google.com.

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:00.270
We'll submit that shortlink.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:05.510
And get this minified
link almost right away.

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:08.860
So we can see that it has
a little [INAUDIBLE] here

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:13.000
that we've generated and
it takes us to google.com.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.550
We could similarly go here
and create the shortlink

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:18.874
and give it a specific
shortlink like "goog."

00:05:18.874 --> 00:05:20.500
Submit and wait.

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:24.400
And oh, we got a 409 that
the shortlink already exists

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:25.840
and go somewhere else.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:29.680
And we've got this
really fun cow

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.860
that's giving us a little
fortune as part of our 404

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:33.710
page.

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:35.560
We'll similarly get
errors if we try

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.110
to go for shortlinks
that don't exist

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:41.380
or if we try to go to
routes that don't exist,

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:43.240
with the appropriate errors.

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:45.930
And get different
fortunes each time.

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:47.680
So now if we move back
to the presentation

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.220
we can talk a little bit
more about the architecture

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:52.850
that we're working with.

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:54.700
So when you come
in, you're going

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:58.210
to be using a front end that's
been built with Express,

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:00.005
and it's run on
Google App Engine.

00:06:00.005 --> 00:06:01.630
That's routing all
of the requests that

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:03.190
are coming from the browser.

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:05.620
Then, depending on the
endpoint that you're hitting,

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:07.035
we're either
creating a shortlink

00:06:07.035 --> 00:06:08.410
or getting a
shortlink, and we're

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:11.922
using a Cloud function to make
calls to the database there.

00:06:11.922 --> 00:06:14.380
And for any of the errors,
we're actually hitting Cloud Run

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:16.255
because we've got some
serverless bash going,

00:06:16.255 --> 00:06:18.140
but we'll show you
that a bit later.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:20.680
One of the things that I really
love about this architecture

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:25.300
is that we've managed to sandbox
the access to the database

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:27.160
from the front-end application.

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:29.530
Through IAM rules, we
can even limit the access

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:31.960
to the database altogether so
that it's only the function

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:34.108
service accounts
that can call it.

00:06:34.108 --> 00:06:35.650
And we're also making
sure that we're

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:38.600
sanitizing the data before
it ever gets to the database.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:41.350
So there's no way
from that front end

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:44.770
to directly access
the database at all.

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:46.450
Now the first
product that we want

00:06:46.450 --> 00:06:48.630
to dig into a little bit
more is Cloud Functions.

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:51.130
Naomi, would you like to tell
us a little bit more about it?

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:52.780
NAOMI SEYFER: So
in Cloud Functions,

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:55.743
you write code to do one thing.

00:06:55.743 --> 00:06:57.910
You don't have to worry
about your request plumbing.

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:02.830
That's handled by the Cloud
Functions framework for you.

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:06.370
And your dependencies are
resolved in the cloud,

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:08.590
so you don't worry about
dependency management.

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:10.480
You don't worry about
request plumbing.

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:11.950
And you can set
your functions up

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:14.410
to trigger by a
variety of things.

00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:17.410
So you can set them up to
trigger by someone creating

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:19.280
an object in a storage bucket.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:22.480
You can set them up to
trigger by someone publishing

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:25.090
something to a
cloud pub/sub topic,

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:27.580
or by HTTP requests
from the outside,

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:31.250
or from your cloud, which
is what we've done here.

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:33.850
And you've got no worries
about concurrency.

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:35.710
You only have one
threat of execution

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:37.010
in your Cloud Function at once.

00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:39.520
So it's really
the easiest to use

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:44.170
of our serverless
compute environments.

00:07:44.170 --> 00:07:46.412
At the same time,
in Cloud Functions

00:07:46.412 --> 00:07:48.620
your language selection is
a little bit more limited.

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:52.360
We've only got Node, Python,
and Go for that right now.

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:55.660
And you have to code in
a way that's specifically

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:56.930
intended for Cloud Function.

00:07:56.930 --> 00:08:01.390
So if you've got a larger
Express.js or Django web

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:05.440
app that you want to
bring into serverless,

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:08.080
Cloud Functions might not
be your choice for that.

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:09.610
That would need
serious refactoring

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:13.000
to work in Cloud Functions.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:15.550
And the flip side of not
worrying about concurrency

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:18.070
is you don't have
access to concurrency.

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:21.970
You can't say give me a hundred
requests that run at once,

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:24.100
I can run a hundred threads.

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:28.000
You're limited to one
request at once per instance.

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:35.590
So Myles will walk you through
some of the Cloud Functions

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:36.772
we've got.

00:08:36.772 --> 00:08:38.230
MYLES BORINS: So
the first function

00:08:38.230 --> 00:08:39.968
that we created was
createShortlink.

00:08:39.968 --> 00:08:41.510
This is the function
that we're going

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:45.100
to call to make new shortlinks
or, alternatively, fail

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:46.390
if it doesn't work.

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:49.990
It takes a URL and a
shortlink as a parameter

00:08:49.990 --> 00:08:52.610
and it stores it
in the database.

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:57.940
The next function that we
created was getShortlink.

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:03.560
It takes a shortlink
as a parameter,

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:05.680
and then it gives
you back the URL

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:08.090
itself that you're
supposed to go to.

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:11.505
So let's take a look
at this turtle here

00:09:11.505 --> 00:09:12.880
and what they're
asking us to do.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:14.780
It's telling us
to go to the demo,

00:09:14.780 --> 00:09:17.500
and I always listen to
what turtles tell me to do.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:20.390
So here we're in the
Cloud console again,

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:22.630
and this is the landing
page for functions.

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:24.790
We've got the
createShortlink function

00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:27.910
and the getURL function.

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:29.560
Let's dig into the
create URL function

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:30.945
and see what's going on here.

00:09:30.945 --> 00:09:32.320
The first thing
that we'll notice

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:35.500
is we've got some really
great insight into the usage.

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:37.210
We can see the most
recent invocation.

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:39.760
That was the demo that I
just showed you earlier.

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:42.100
We can see the usage
over the last 30 days,

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:44.920
and we can see the day
that I sent this service

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:47.080
to all my friends
to try to break it

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:49.177
before I used it onstage.

00:09:49.177 --> 00:09:51.010
We also get access to
a couple other things.

00:09:51.010 --> 00:09:52.593
We have the trigger
itself right here,

00:09:52.593 --> 00:09:53.860
so we can click this link.

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:56.110
And you can see we get
an error, because we

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:58.450
haven't sent the right request.

00:09:58.450 --> 00:10:02.970
We can see the source code as
well, by clicking on Source.

00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:05.640
If we look at
index.js here, we can

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:07.410
see there's a couple
helper functions

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:10.050
that we've made for
interacting with the database.

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:13.020
And here you can
see the general way

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:15.780
in which we create functions.

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:18.210
I'm exporting the function
with the name of what

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:21.060
my service will be
called, and that export

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:22.750
is an asynchronous function.

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:25.182
This will allow me to
use the awake keyword.

00:10:25.182 --> 00:10:26.640
I don't know-- how
many people here

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:29.870
are familiar with
JavaScript and async/await?

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:31.200
Oh, very cool!

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:34.767
I think it's made the language
infinitely better to work with.

00:10:34.767 --> 00:10:36.600
And a really cool thing
with Cloud Functions

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:39.240
is you can export
async functions, which

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:42.720
makes it far simpler to start
doing some asynchronous stuff.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:45.630
You get a request and a
response for an HTP function

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:49.360
and it's more or less the exact
same contract as an Express app

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:51.093
if you're used to using Express.

00:10:51.093 --> 00:10:53.010
So here we have a couple
different things that

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:56.010
we're doing to make sure that
we've gotten a good request

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:59.040
and sending proper
warnings when we haven't.

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:02.610
And then when we actually make
the call to the database here,

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:04.860
we can send the right
information if the result was

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:06.750
successful and
warn if it wasn't.

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:09.090
Now we also, in the
Cloud Functions thing

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:13.090
here, have the ability of
testing the function itself.

00:11:13.090 --> 00:11:19.970
So we can go ahead and give
the long link to google.com

00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:23.250
and try to test that function
and see what happens.

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:25.880
And it gave us back a
warning that we should also

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:27.540
include the shortlink.

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:29.510
So let's do that
right now, and we'll

00:11:29.510 --> 00:11:31.900
give it the short link google.

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:34.210
And we can test the
function, and it successfully

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:37.010
returned the shortlink, which
means that the service worked.

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:40.430
So if we go back really
quickly to getURL.

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:42.700
And we can jump
right to testing it.

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:51.710
We can call getURL with
the shortlink google.

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:53.570
We can test the
function, and we can

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:56.780
see that we got back
the URL that we created.

00:11:56.780 --> 00:11:59.210
One of the things you will
notice with this service

00:11:59.210 --> 00:12:02.450
is that we're not baking
in any of the hostnames.

00:12:02.450 --> 00:12:05.540
So what's really cool
about this is this service

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:08.240
can sit on top of any
number of different URLs,

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:10.760
and the database could
even transfer around

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:12.888
for other services.

00:12:12.888 --> 00:12:14.930
So that's it to show right
now for the functions.

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:17.117
If we go back to the
slide deck, we're

00:12:17.117 --> 00:12:19.200
going to look at the next
technology that we used,

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:21.530
which was App Engine, which
we used for the front end.

00:12:21.530 --> 00:12:24.440
And Naomi will walk us through
a little bit about App Engine

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:27.680
and why you might want to
use that specific product.

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:30.280
NAOMI SEYFER: Whereas
Functions was one operation

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:31.100
and you're done--

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:34.960
you just do one function,
return the response to that,

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:36.460
and done--

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:38.290
in App Engine you
write a service

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:40.000
that can deal with
a variety of paths

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:42.840
and can do a variety
of related operations.

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:48.170
You still have a lot of
the benefits of functions,

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:53.840
like the automatic security,
patching of libraries.

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:57.840
The libraries are still
resolved in the cloud.

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:01.400
But you have a few more
language runtimes available.

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:04.910
We have Java, Python,
Go, PHP, and Node.

00:13:04.910 --> 00:13:07.760
And you can bring your
own web application

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:10.010
framework or mobile back-end
application framework.

00:13:10.010 --> 00:13:12.700
If you want to write in Django,
you can write in Django, Flask,

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:13.698
you can write in Flask.

00:13:13.698 --> 00:13:15.740
Can you tell that I'm a
Python person rather than

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:19.060
a Node person, mostly?

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:22.640
And you can have the
idiomatic language environment

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:24.110
to what you're used to.

00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:26.570
So you write the
code that you're

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:30.200
used to deploying to any
other kind of situation.

00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:33.440
You write that.

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:35.870
You send the code up
to us in the cloud.

00:13:35.870 --> 00:13:37.820
We resolve the
dependencies for you

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:42.680
in a language-idiomatic way,
and we make your code run,

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:45.260
and we scale it automatically,
and do the serverless thing

00:13:45.260 --> 00:13:50.140
for you in all the ways that
I described serverless before.

00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:52.060
Myles will describe
the front end

00:13:52.060 --> 00:13:53.872
that we wrote in App Engine.

00:13:53.872 --> 00:13:56.080
MYLES BORINS: So we have a
couple of different routes

00:13:56.080 --> 00:13:58.780
that we're handling in App
Engine with the Express app

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:01.690
that we built. The first
route is just slash.

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:04.370
It's what happens at the
root, and for that we serve

00:14:04.370 --> 00:14:06.740
a form for a new shortlink.

00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:10.930
The next route that we
have is a post to /link.

00:14:10.930 --> 00:14:12.400
This is the endpoint
that we post

00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:14.860
to to create new
shortlinks that will then

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:16.810
proxy that request
to the Cloud Function

00:14:16.810 --> 00:14:18.710
that I was showing earlier.

00:14:18.710 --> 00:14:23.290
Finally, we have a
post to get shortlink.

00:14:23.290 --> 00:14:25.870
This is very similar to
just get on the root,

00:14:25.870 --> 00:14:29.380
except we catch any other
words that are there

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:31.910
to be used as a parameter
for figuring out,

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:33.910
hey, what shortlink
are we asking for.

00:14:33.910 --> 00:14:35.740
And then we serve the
appropriate redirect

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:38.000
for the browser.

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:40.630
So I think the turtle's
telling us it's time

00:14:40.630 --> 00:14:43.780
to show off a demo again.

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:45.507
So here we're in the
App Engine console.

00:14:45.507 --> 00:14:47.590
And if you are trying to
figure out how to get it,

00:14:47.590 --> 00:14:48.965
if you click the
Navigation menu,

00:14:48.965 --> 00:14:50.560
here you can see
that we've pinned it.

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:53.770
But if you just were to scroll
