We can see services.

00:15:09.310 --> 00:15:11.590
We have one default service
that's deployed right now,

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:13.850
but you can deploy
multiple services,

00:15:13.850 --> 00:15:16.360
multiple independent
App Engine instances all

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:17.260
in the same project.

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:18.820
You're not limited to one.

00:15:18.820 --> 00:15:20.860
We have various versions.

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:22.360
These are all the
different versions

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:24.080
that we've deployed
of this application.

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:26.450
As you can see, we've made
some changes over time.

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:28.660
One of the things that
I really love about this

00:15:28.660 --> 00:15:31.420
is the traffic allocation
bit that you can see here.

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:33.940
It makes it really intuitive
and straightforward,

00:15:33.940 --> 00:15:36.760
with a couple clicks
to split traffic

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:39.040
across a couple
different instances.

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:42.050
One time where I find this
extremely useful is hey,

00:15:42.050 --> 00:15:44.560
we've made a small
change and we're not 100%

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:46.720
whether or not this is
going to break some people.

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:49.810
You can do a new version,
not automatically deploy

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:52.930
its production, and just
write about maybe 10% or 5%

00:15:52.930 --> 00:15:54.700
of traffic to that service.

00:15:54.700 --> 00:15:57.130
You can even run that
service with special headers

00:15:57.130 --> 00:16:00.130
in the logger so it's easier
to see exactly what's going on

00:16:00.130 --> 00:16:02.690
and progressively roll out
that service over time,

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:06.310
instead of just transitioning
all the traffic at once.

00:16:06.310 --> 00:16:09.010
You'll be able to see there's
