00:21:17.685 --> 00:21:19.310
we had one last
service that we haven't

00:21:19.310 --> 00:21:21.260
talked about yet, which
is the one that I'm

00:21:21.260 --> 00:21:24.050
most excited about, which
is Cloud Run, the product

00:21:24.050 --> 00:21:25.177
that Naomi helped build.

00:21:25.177 --> 00:21:27.260
So Naomi, do you want to
tell us a little bit more

00:21:27.260 --> 00:21:28.490
about what Cloud Run is?

00:21:28.490 --> 00:21:29.282
NAOMI SEYFER: Sure.

00:21:29.282 --> 00:21:32.540
Cloud Run-- whereas
in App Engine

00:21:32.540 --> 00:21:35.510
you write with your favorite
framework, in Cloud Run

00:21:35.510 --> 00:21:37.310
you can include
arbitrary software.

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:40.370
Cloud Run is our serverless
container runtime.

00:21:40.370 --> 00:21:43.190
So with containers-- these
are just your normal Docker

00:21:43.190 --> 00:21:45.410
containers, for people who
are familiar with that.

00:21:45.410 --> 00:21:49.310
You can use any language,
any libraries you want,

00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:52.310
any binaries you want if
you have software that comes

00:21:52.310 --> 00:21:54.230
prepackaged does a binary.

00:21:54.230 --> 00:21:58.100
And you can use the whole
ecosystem of Docker base images

00:21:58.100 --> 00:22:00.770
that already exists in
places like Docker Hub.

00:22:00.770 --> 00:22:05.990
So you can have an Ubuntu
base image, or even

00:22:05.990 --> 00:22:08.160
a Node.js base image.

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:11.150
And Docker containers have
become the industry standard

00:22:11.150 --> 00:22:14.900
for software
transmission, basically.

00:22:14.900 --> 00:22:18.260
Each Docker container
contains all

00:22:18.260 --> 00:22:20.210
that's necessary for
the software on it

00:22:20.210 --> 00:22:22.438
to pretend it's running
in its own computer,

00:22:22.438 --> 00:22:23.480
though it's not actually.

00:22:23.480 --> 00:22:26.210
That's all operating
system fakery

00:22:26.210 --> 00:22:28.040
that gives it that impression.

00:22:28.040 --> 00:22:31.750
It's much faster than
provisioning a VM.

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:35.560
So the product we have for our
container runtime is Cloud Run.

00:22:35.560 --> 00:22:37.420
Container to
production in seconds

00:22:37.420 --> 00:22:39.760
while still being
serverless, without having

00:22:39.760 --> 00:22:43.000
to provision anything,
or anything like that.

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:48.770
It scales up fast and down to
zero when you're not using it.

00:22:48.770 --> 00:22:52.840
And you pay for the exact
usage that you're using.

00:22:52.840 --> 00:22:55.480
So we actually have two
versions of Cloud Run.

00:22:55.480 --> 00:23:01.010
Cloud Run, fully managed is the
one that we mostly talk about,

00:23:01.010 --> 00:23:03.310
but there's also
Cloud Run on GKE.

00:23:03.310 --> 00:23:06.710
Cloud Run, fully managed,
Google is your ops team.

00:23:06.710 --> 00:23:10.630
Google does all the management
of scaling up and down for you.

00:23:10.630 --> 00:23:13.450
It uses our
proprietary back end.

00:23:13.450 --> 00:23:16.480
And Cloud Run on GKE
is an alternative form

00:23:16.480 --> 00:23:20.360
of Cloud Run that has the exact
same developer experience.

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:22.180
You still use the same
commands to deploy.

00:23:22.180 --> 00:23:24.240
It feels the same to deploy.

00:23:24.240 --> 00:23:27.580
It still scales up
fast and down to zero.

00:23:27.580 --> 00:23:30.220
But it's actually
running on a GKE cluster

00:23:30.220 --> 00:23:32.440
that you or your company manage.

00:23:32.440 --> 00:23:35.800
So if you have an
ops team that demands

00:23:35.800 --> 00:23:39.310
that they have full control over
wherever your stuff is running,

00:23:39.310 --> 00:23:43.030
or a platform team that
demands full control,

00:23:43.030 --> 00:23:47.830
they can provision a
Cloud Run on GKE cluster.

00:23:47.830 --> 00:23:52.070
Your organization
pays for the cluster,

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:55.790
and then you have the same
developer experience for Cloud

00:23:55.790 --> 00:23:57.680
Run and it still
feels serverless

00:23:57.680 --> 00:24:01.020
to the developers in
your organization.

00:24:01.020 --> 00:24:05.480
In Cloud Run, fully managed,
you pay exactly per use

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:08.130
at the 100 millisecond
time scale.

00:24:08.130 --> 00:24:11.900
So you rent CPU,
you rent memory,

00:24:11.900 --> 00:24:16.040
and you rent request handling.

00:24:16.040 --> 00:24:19.670
And then in Cloud
Run on GKE, you

00:24:19.670 --> 00:24:23.330
can do basically the same
thing from the perspective

00:24:23.330 --> 00:24:26.810
of a development team
within an organization.

00:24:26.810 --> 00:24:29.930
You can use the GKE
Usage Metering feature

00:24:29.930 --> 00:24:31.760
so that the development
team can get

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:35.660
billed by the operations team
in this same serverless way.

00:24:37.962 --> 00:24:39.420
MYLES BORINS: So
when we're talking

00:24:39.420 --> 00:24:42.180
about deploying and
building for Cloud Run,

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:44.040
there's a couple of
different ways to do it.

00:24:44.040 --> 00:24:46.230
The base is that you
have a Docker image,

00:24:46.230 --> 00:24:48.450
and that Docker image
is on GCR, which

00:24:48.450 --> 00:24:50.400
is Google Cloud repository.

00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:52.780
That's the right
name, right, Naomi?

00:24:52.780 --> 00:24:54.530
NAOMI SEYFER: Google
Container Repository.

00:24:54.530 --> 00:24:55.050
MYLES BORINS:
Container Repository.

00:24:55.050 --> 00:24:56.080
I knew I was wrong.

00:24:56.080 --> 00:24:57.840
That's why I asked.

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:02.040
So with Cloud Build you
can create those images

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:04.020
automatically
based on Git repos.

00:25:04.020 --> 00:25:05.910
You can also do it
yourself with Docker,

00:25:05.910 --> 00:25:07.860
build locally and push upstream.

00:25:07.860 --> 00:25:11.220
And you can also do that within
your own CI/CD infrastructure.

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:15.270
As long as the image is on
GCR, Google Container Registry,

00:25:15.270 --> 00:25:18.400
you'll be able to deploy
it with Cloud Run.

00:25:18.400 --> 00:25:21.210
And you may have been asking
what is a Container Registry?

00:25:21.210 --> 00:25:24.790
A Container Registry is a place
to store container images,

00:25:24.790 --> 00:25:26.860
publicly or privately.

00:25:26.860 --> 00:25:30.400
It works via using the
Docker push and pull command.

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:32.630
For GCR, you pay
only for the storage.

00:25:32.630 --> 00:25:34.090
So when you push
a container, you

00:25:34.090 --> 00:25:35.548
can kind of think
of that container

00:25:35.548 --> 00:25:36.880
like a series of tarballs.

00:25:36.880 --> 00:25:38.710
You're storing that
inside of a bucket.

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:41.620
You're only getting billed
for the on-disk space

00:25:41.620 --> 00:25:43.660
that you're taking up.

00:25:43.660 --> 00:25:45.910
One of the great things that
we do offer as well, when

00:25:45.910 --> 00:25:48.130
you push containers to
our Container Registry

00:25:48.130 --> 00:25:51.220
is that you get container
vulnerability scanning.

00:25:51.220 --> 00:25:54.310
So we had mentioned
before that for App Engine

00:25:54.310 --> 00:25:58.460
and for Cloud Functions, because
everything from the platform,

00:25:58.460 --> 00:26:01.690
such as Node.js or Ruby,
down is managed by Google.

00:26:01.690 --> 00:26:03.940
If there's new versions
of Node, or new versions

00:26:03.940 --> 00:26:05.980
of the operating
system, or patches

00:26:05.980 --> 00:26:08.050
that need to be applied
to system libraries,

00:26:08.050 --> 00:26:10.800
we do that all for you under
the hood, and the next time we

00:26:10.800 --> 00:26:13.240
spin up your containers
they just have those updates

00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:14.293
automatically.

00:26:14.293 --> 00:26:16.210
You still need to manage
your own dependencies

00:26:16.210 --> 00:26:17.690
within your application.

00:26:17.690 --> 00:26:19.403
But when you're
deploying containers,

00:26:19.403 --> 00:26:20.570
you've contained everything.

00:26:20.570 --> 00:26:21.945
Everything is in
there and you're

00:26:21.945 --> 00:26:23.218
maintaining that yourself.

00:26:23.218 --> 00:26:24.760
So you're responsible
for making sure

00:26:24.760 --> 00:26:26.800
that all that stuff is secure.

00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:29.500
That's part of the
spectrum that Naomi

00:26:29.500 --> 00:26:30.970
was talking about earlier.

00:26:30.970 --> 00:26:33.770
You have more control, but that
comes with more responsibility.

00:26:33.770 --> 00:26:34.270
But--

00:26:34.270 --> 00:26:35.980
NAOMI SEYFER: We
still got your kernel.

00:26:35.980 --> 00:26:39.220
We still got your
kernel in Cloud Run,

00:26:39.220 --> 00:26:43.458
but your libraries, you rely
on the vulnerability scanner.

00:26:43.458 --> 00:26:45.250
MYLES BORINS: And the
vulnerability scanner

00:26:45.250 --> 00:26:48.040
will let if your images
that you have in GCR

00:26:48.040 --> 00:26:49.780
have any
vulnerabilities in them.

00:26:49.780 --> 00:26:54.690
I'm personally a big
fan of that technology.

00:26:54.690 --> 00:26:57.110
So when you want
to go to deploy,

00:26:57.110 --> 00:26:58.910
this is the command
that you would run.

00:26:58.910 --> 00:27:02.840
And you start with the registry.

00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:06.020
And you gcloud beta run deploy,
because the thing is still

00:27:06.020 --> 00:27:07.070
in beta.

00:27:07.070 --> 00:27:09.650
Image, and then the
link to the registry.

00:27:09.650 --> 00:27:12.080
Cloud Run takes that
Container Registry image,

00:27:12.080 --> 00:27:14.930
deploys it, and gives you
a URL that you can then

00:27:14.930 --> 00:27:18.450
visit to see your
service in production.

00:27:18.450 --> 00:27:22.440
And similarly to App Engine, you
can also setup custom domains.

00:27:22.440 --> 00:27:26.070
And those custom domains will
have managed SSL for you.

00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:30.300
So your whole deployment can
just be gcloud beta run deploy.

00:27:30.300 --> 00:27:33.330
And if any of you have any
experience with Cloud Build,

00:27:33.330 --> 00:27:35.010
it's fairly intuitive
to set that up

00:27:35.010 --> 00:27:37.920
to be automated, based
on pushes to a Git repo,

00:27:37.920 --> 00:27:39.630
or all sorts of
other triggers that

00:27:39.630 --> 00:27:43.550
can set this off to automate
continuous deployment for you.

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:48.980
NAOMI SEYFER: Cloud
Run is based on Knative

00:27:48.980 --> 00:27:53.300
which is a common API
and runtime environment

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:55.100
for serverless containers.

00:27:55.100 --> 00:27:58.490
So one of the wonderful
things about Cloud Run

00:27:58.490 --> 00:28:02.330
is if you obey the contract
for what a container should

00:28:02.330 --> 00:28:06.620
and shouldn't do, you
can port that container

00:28:06.620 --> 00:28:10.380
to anywhere that runs Knative.

00:28:10.380 --> 00:28:13.230
Now you might have to
dig yourself access holes

00:28:13.230 --> 00:28:19.290
to any other Google Cloud
services that you use,

00:28:19.290 --> 00:28:21.330
but if you follow the
container contract,

00:28:21.330 --> 00:28:25.860
it should be reasonably portable
between Knative deployments.

00:28:25.860 --> 00:28:30.810
So Cloud Run is a kind
of Knative deployment,

00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:32.310
Cloud Run on GKE.

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:35.160
And Knative is actually
open-source software,

00:28:35.160 --> 00:28:39.870
so you can take Knative and
install it on a Kubernetes

00:28:39.870 --> 00:28:41.140
cluster of your choice.

00:28:41.140 --> 00:28:44.850
The reference implementation
is based on Kubernetes.

00:28:44.850 --> 00:28:49.320
Or go to another
Knative cloud provider

00:28:49.320 --> 00:28:51.840
and take your same
container and put it there.

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:55.080
So you might have heard
about problems with lock-in

00:28:55.080 --> 00:28:57.080
and locking into one cloud.

00:28:57.080 --> 00:28:58.890
Cloud Run is what you
might want to choose

00:28:58.890 --> 00:29:01.620
if you want to run a
multi-cloud deployment

00:29:01.620 --> 00:29:04.620
or not have to
worry about lock-in.

00:29:04.620 --> 00:29:06.870
You can actually
take your container

00:29:06.870 --> 00:29:09.780
and leave Google with it and
get a very similar environment.

00:29:09.780 --> 00:29:11.880
Not only the
runtime environment,

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:14.970
but also the deployment
workflow is both the same.

00:29:14.970 --> 00:29:20.307
It uses the same API and the
same runtime environment.

00:29:20.307 --> 00:29:22.390
The container runtime
contract that I've mentioned

00:29:22.390 --> 00:29:23.770
has a few things in it.

00:29:23.770 --> 00:29:26.560
I'm just going to
give some highlights.

00:29:26.560 --> 00:29:29.200
This is the Knative
container runtime contract.

00:29:29.200 --> 00:29:33.070
The first thing is you need
to make your container have

00:29:33.070 --> 00:29:35.360
a stateless service in it.

00:29:35.360 --> 00:29:36.820
So if you need a
database, you need

00:29:36.820 --> 00:29:39.730
to put that out of your
container and connect to it.

00:29:39.730 --> 00:29:42.670
If you need access to
files, you need to put them

00:29:42.670 --> 00:29:45.400
in some kind of cloud bucket.

00:29:45.400 --> 00:29:48.670
Google Cloud Storage is one
of the possibilities there,

00:29:48.670 --> 00:29:51.190
but basically
anywhere on the cloud

00:29:51.190 --> 00:29:53.135
that you can store your files.

00:29:53.135 --> 00:29:55.260
MYLES BORINS: Naomi, just
a quick question for you.

00:29:55.260 --> 00:29:56.560
Statelessness,
isn't that something

00:29:56.560 --> 00:29:58.300
we need to worry about all
the time in serverless?

00:29:58.300 --> 00:29:59.170
NAOMI SEYFER: It
is something you

00:29:59.170 --> 00:30:01.128
need to worry about all
the time in serverless.

00:30:01.128 --> 00:30:04.330
Both Functions and App Engine
do require statelessness

00:30:04.330 --> 00:30:06.940
in their operation.

00:30:06.940 --> 00:30:09.340
The next thing is
whatever your container

00:30:09.340 --> 00:30:13.060
is, it needs to listen for
HTTP requests on $PORT.

00:30:13.060 --> 00:30:15.220
That's how stuff gets
in, and the response

00:30:15.220 --> 00:30:17.860
is how stuff gets out.

00:30:17.860 --> 00:30:23.380
If you like a little
thing is in Cloud Run,

00:30:23.380 --> 00:30:26.120
mostly that's the same
port all the time.

00:30:26.120 --> 00:30:28.000
But if you want to
be able to be porting

00:30:28.000 --> 00:30:31.360
from Cloud Run to other
Knative installations,

00:30:31.360 --> 00:30:34.000
you want to listen on $PORT
because they might set that

00:30:34.000 --> 00:30:36.980
to something else.

00:30:36.980 --> 00:30:39.350
And another thing that
you have to worry about

00:30:39.350 --> 00:30:44.090
is not using CPU
outside of requests.

00:30:44.090 --> 00:30:47.180
You're only guaranteed
to get access to the CPU

00:30:47.180 --> 00:30:49.220
while you're answering
your requests.

00:30:49.220 --> 00:30:51.980
The system reserves the right
to scale you down at any time

00:30:51.980 --> 00:30:55.380
when you're not
answering a request.

00:30:55.380 --> 00:30:58.940
There's a few other things about
handling signals and things

00:30:58.940 --> 00:31:02.270
like that, and that's
documented with Knative

00:31:02.270 --> 00:31:03.380
and with Cloud Run.

00:31:03.380 --> 00:31:06.470
You can look that up
on our documentation.

00:31:06.470 --> 00:31:10.000
So in our container,
anything Unix goes.

00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:12.880
So we decided to just use a
few old school Unix programs

00:31:12.880 --> 00:31:17.800
and write a serverless bash
program for handling our 404's.

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:20.770
So we use the Unix
program fortune

00:31:20.770 --> 00:31:23.890
to pipe it to the
Unix program cowsay

00:31:23.890 --> 00:31:25.870
and serve the results
with the Unix program

00:31:25.870 --> 00:31:28.340
netcat and its ncat variety.

00:31:28.340 --> 00:31:28.875
Hi, netcat.

00:31:33.300 --> 00:31:33.800
All right.

00:31:33.800 --> 00:31:34.342
Let's see it.

00:31:38.030 --> 00:31:41.380
So over here we're in
the Cloud Run dashboard,

00:31:41.380 --> 00:31:44.810
and we have one service
called fortune-telling-cow.

00:31:44.810 --> 00:31:49.850
And I'm going to click on
that to take a peek at it.

00:31:49.850 --> 00:31:51.510
And it has a lot of revisions.

00:31:51.510 --> 00:31:53.450
And very similar to
the App Engine world,

00:31:53.450 --> 00:31:56.960
you can see all your revisions.

00:31:56.960 --> 00:31:58.580
You can manipulate them.

00:31:58.580 --> 00:32:01.570
You can even go to the YAML view
and see the Kubernetes-style

00:32:01.570 --> 00:32:03.230
YAML that you have.

00:32:03.230 --> 00:32:05.030
That has to do
with a Knative API,

00:32:05.030 --> 00:32:08.590
and you can get all
the details there.

00:32:08.590 --> 00:32:12.540
So I'm going to go
over to the Cloud Shell

00:32:12.540 --> 00:32:14.790
and show how a workflow
might work here

00:32:14.790 --> 00:32:17.710
and how our app works.

00:32:17.710 --> 00:32:23.430
Here's our repository
here with our serve.sh.

00:32:23.430 --> 00:32:28.140
I'm going to increase the
size of this window briefly.

00:32:28.140 --> 00:32:30.900
And you can see that
the serve.sh sets

00:32:30.900 --> 00:32:33.030
a port if it isn't provided.

00:32:35.550 --> 00:32:38.070
But we're going to connect to
the port environment variable.

00:32:38.070 --> 00:32:42.360
We trap SIGINT to kill things.

00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:46.080
And then what we do is we
start the program ncat,

00:32:46.080 --> 00:32:50.400
tell it to listen and keep
listening for more connections

00:32:50.400 --> 00:32:54.000
even after the first
one on the port $PORT.

00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:56.053
See also the container contract.

00:32:56.053 --> 00:32:58.470
And then whenever it gets a
connection, exec the following

00:32:58.470 --> 00:32:59.550
things--

00:32:59.550 --> 00:33:03.360
echo a beginning of
an HTTP response,

00:33:03.360 --> 00:33:06.000
give it a content type
header of plain text,

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:08.670
and then pipe the result
of fortune to cowsay.

00:33:11.360 --> 00:33:16.010
And so let's look at what
happens when I run that.

00:33:16.010 --> 00:33:18.650
To run that, what I need to do
is I need to do a docker build.

00:33:22.410 --> 00:33:29.090
And I'm going to give it a
tag of the image environment

00:33:29.090 --> 00:33:29.590
variable.

00:33:32.220 --> 00:33:32.790
Ah, right.

00:33:32.790 --> 00:33:33.290
Dot.

00:33:36.035 --> 00:33:36.535
Oh.

00:33:40.490 --> 00:33:43.140
I need to be in the right
directory before I do that.

00:33:43.140 --> 00:33:47.500
And I give it the image
environment variable.

00:33:47.500 --> 00:33:51.810
And as soon as
this finishes, I'm

00:33:51.810 --> 00:33:54.370
going to show you what's in
the image environment variable.

00:33:54.370 --> 00:33:55.691
It's not complicated.

00:33:58.460 --> 00:34:02.960
It's just where I'm going
to put this thing on GCR.

00:34:02.960 --> 00:34:03.460
OK.

00:34:03.460 --> 00:34:11.260
So now I want to do docker run
--rm to finish when I'm done.

00:34:11.260 --> 00:34:20.300
-it so it's interactive and
gets the terminal, and -p.

00:34:20.300 --> 00:34:25.300
And then I want to
map port 8080 to 8080.

00:34:25.300 --> 00:34:28.137
And so this is just how
you might run an image.

00:34:28.137 --> 00:34:29.929
And now we can see over
in the Cloud Shell,

00:34:29.929 --> 00:34:34.012
we can preview this on port 8080
to see what's going on here.

00:34:34.012 --> 00:34:35.929
And we have our wonderful
fortune-telling cow.

00:34:40.170 --> 00:34:42.719
Kill that window.

00:34:42.719 --> 00:34:45.360
Control-C out of this business.

00:34:45.360 --> 00:34:46.020
All right.

00:34:46.020 --> 00:34:50.187
So in our Cloud Run workflow,
I might want to change this.

00:34:50.187 --> 00:34:52.020
Let's say I don't want
a turtle to say this.

00:34:52.020 --> 00:34:54.312
I want a different-- or I
don't want a cow to say this.

00:34:54.312 --> 00:34:57.960
I want a different kind
of cow, which is turtle.

00:35:01.400 --> 00:35:03.780
In terms of cow, say
turtle is a kind of cow.

00:35:03.780 --> 00:35:04.280
Who knew?

00:35:08.790 --> 00:35:11.220
And I want to save this file.

00:35:11.220 --> 00:35:12.970
And now I want to do
another docker build.

00:35:16.740 --> 00:35:18.840
That happens very
fast because I'm only

00:35:18.840 --> 00:35:24.180
changing the one layer of this
Docker file that I have here.

00:35:24.180 --> 00:35:27.600
By the way, let's take a
brief look at the Docker file.

00:35:27.600 --> 00:35:31.530
All this Docker file says
it's based on Alpine Linux.

00:35:31.530 --> 00:35:35.200
We want to install the
few programs that we use.

00:35:35.200 --> 00:35:38.340
Here's the installation of ncat,
the installation of fortune.

00:35:38.340 --> 00:35:40.810
We have a more complicated
installation of cowsay

00:35:40.810 --> 00:35:43.470
because we're installing
it from a Git repository.

00:35:43.470 --> 00:35:47.930
Copy every file from this
directory into the image.

00:35:47.930 --> 00:35:49.842
Build a set of
wholesome fortunes,

00:35:49.842 --> 00:35:52.300
because we don't want to demo
unwholesome fortunes for you.

00:35:56.430 --> 00:35:58.560
Set up our path
environment variable,

00:35:58.560 --> 00:36:02.840
and set the command to be
this serve.sh that we viewed.

00:36:02.840 --> 00:36:03.840
MYLES BORINS: Fun story.

00:36:03.840 --> 00:36:06.067
It was originally
called server, but Naomi

00:36:06.067 --> 00:36:07.650
noticed that we
wouldn't be serverless

00:36:07.650 --> 00:36:10.620
if we called it server.sh.

00:36:10.620 --> 00:36:12.060
NAOMI SEYFER: So
it's just serve.

00:36:12.060 --> 00:36:14.470
There is no server involved.

00:36:14.470 --> 00:36:18.700
So now that we've built
that Docker container,

00:36:18.700 --> 00:36:25.220
we can push it to GCR,
docker push $IMAGE.

00:36:25.220 --> 00:36:28.680
So that pushes all of those
layers that we've created.

00:36:28.680 --> 00:36:32.840
One of them already exists,
probably the base image.

00:36:32.840 --> 00:36:36.410
And as soon as we
get our prompt back,

00:36:36.410 --> 00:36:46.350
we can do gcloud
beta run deploy,

00:36:46.350 --> 00:36:50.074
and the name of our service
is fortune-telling-cow.

00:36:57.180 --> 00:37:00.695
And dash dash image
and our image.

00:37:03.265 --> 00:37:05.120
Cool.

00:37:05.120 --> 00:37:09.050
Let's deploy that to
our Cloud Run service.

00:37:09.050 --> 00:37:11.330
And you see, it tells
you what it's doing,

00:37:11.330 --> 00:37:14.060
what it's deploying.

00:37:14.060 --> 00:37:15.930
I'm actually in charge
of the command line,

00:37:15.930 --> 00:37:18.900
so I'm proud of all
this interactiveness.

00:37:18.900 --> 00:37:21.870
It health-checks it before it
migrates any traffic to it.

00:37:21.870 --> 00:37:24.680
So if, as soon as you send
any request to your thing

00:37:24.680 --> 00:37:27.170
it crashes, it won't
migrate any traffic.

00:37:27.170 --> 00:37:29.100
It will let you know
that there's an error.

00:37:29.100 --> 00:37:29.600
Cool.

00:37:29.600 --> 00:37:32.210
It's deployed and
is serving traffic.

00:37:32.210 --> 00:37:36.665
So we can go and
check out this URL.

00:37:42.110 --> 00:37:43.595
Fortune telling cow.

00:37:47.570 --> 00:37:48.370
Oh, dear.

00:37:48.370 --> 00:37:49.370
Did I misspelled turtle?

00:37:59.800 --> 00:38:01.100
I'm sorry, -f turtle.

00:38:04.530 --> 00:38:07.120
Now I need to docker build
and deploy very quickly.

00:38:10.938 --> 00:38:13.480
MYLES BORINS: I think this is
a really great example, though,

00:38:13.480 --> 00:38:16.020
of how quick this workflow is.

00:38:16.020 --> 00:38:18.780
And for those of you who have
experience with GCF versus App

00:38:18.780 --> 00:38:20.297
Engine, it takes
a little bit more

00:38:20.297 --> 00:38:21.880
time because all of
its infrastructure

00:38:21.880 --> 00:38:24.780
is all being managed by us
at once, but just in the time

00:38:24.780 --> 00:38:27.270
that it took me to
stall and talk to you

00:38:27.270 --> 00:38:31.440
all just now, we're almost done
deploying this whole service,

00:38:31.440 --> 00:38:34.002
upgrading all the
traffic, and rerouting it.

00:38:34.002 --> 00:38:35.460
I think it's probably
going to only

00:38:35.460 --> 00:38:36.720
be about another 10 seconds.

00:38:36.720 --> 00:38:37.950
NAOMI SEYFER: We're
at the health check.

00:38:37.950 --> 00:38:40.530
We're routing traffic, assigning
traffic the latest ready

00:38:40.530 --> 00:38:42.832
revision.

00:38:42.832 --> 00:38:44.040
MYLES BORINS: And we're done.

00:38:44.040 --> 00:38:45.873
NAOMI SEYFER: And we're
done, and you should

00:38:45.873 --> 00:38:50.165
be able to hit Refresh on this.

00:38:50.165 --> 00:38:51.040
MYLES BORINS: Turtle.

00:38:51.040 --> 00:38:54.680
NAOMI SEYFER: And it needs
to start up a new instance.

00:38:54.680 --> 00:38:56.235
MYLES BORINS: Turtle.

00:38:56.235 --> 00:38:56.860
Say it with me.

00:38:56.860 --> 00:38:58.590
Turtle.

00:38:58.590 --> 00:38:59.662
NAOMI SEYFER: I'm sorry.

00:38:59.662 --> 00:39:00.370
We have a turtle.

00:39:00.370 --> 00:39:00.780
MYLES BORINS: Turtle!

00:39:00.780 --> 00:39:00.970
NAOMI SEYFER: Here we are.

00:39:00.970 --> 00:39:02.077
We have a turtle.

00:39:02.077 --> 00:39:04.819
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:06.690 --> 00:39:12.330
And now if we go and in
our microservices-based app

00:39:12.330 --> 00:39:14.940
hit something that
we don't know,

00:39:14.940 --> 00:39:19.170
we can see that we have a
turtle instead of the cow

00:39:19.170 --> 00:39:21.560
that Myles showed you before.

00:39:21.560 --> 00:39:23.060
Now if we can go
back to the slides.

00:39:26.880 --> 00:39:28.865
MYLES BORINS: So we've
gone through a handful

00:39:28.865 --> 00:39:30.240
of different
serverless offerings

00:39:30.240 --> 00:39:31.680
that we have at Google.

00:39:31.680 --> 00:39:34.080
We've primarily focused on
our compute offerings, which

00:39:34.080 --> 00:39:35.260
are the ones in the center.

00:39:35.260 --> 00:39:37.740
So that was Cloud
Functions, App Engine,

00:39:37.740 --> 00:39:42.840
and Cloud Run, which is the new,
exciting technology that we've

00:39:42.840 --> 00:39:43.477
got.

00:39:43.477 --> 00:39:45.060
We also showed some
other technologies

00:39:45.060 --> 00:39:47.143
that you may not have the
mental model of thinking

00:39:47.143 --> 00:39:49.860
about as serverless,
such as Cloud SQL, which

00:39:49.860 --> 00:39:52.710
is being managed by us
and can scale to zero.

00:39:52.710 --> 00:39:55.500
Now there is other database
technologies that we have

00:39:55.500 --> 00:39:56.700
that we consider serverless.

00:39:56.700 --> 00:39:59.880
There's messaging
services such as Pub/Sub,

00:39:59.880 --> 00:40:01.740
DevOps services such
as Cloud Scheduler

00:40:01.740 --> 00:40:03.198
that I was mentioning
before, which

00:40:03.198 --> 00:40:04.281
is basically managed cron.

00:40:04.281 --> 00:40:05.781
So if there's
anything that you ever

00:40:05.781 --> 00:40:07.420
wanted to do that's
based on a timing,

00:40:07.420 --> 00:40:09.210
it just makes it so easy to do.

00:40:09.210 --> 00:40:11.970
We have machine
learning APIs as well,

00:40:11.970 --> 00:40:15.810
as well as stuff around
Assistant and Chat.

00:40:15.810 --> 00:40:19.350
But you can build large
services with all of those.

00:40:19.350 --> 00:40:21.510
A really quick summary, though.

00:40:21.510 --> 00:40:24.270
Functions, do one thing.

00:40:24.270 --> 00:40:26.370
Apps, do many things.

00:40:26.370 --> 00:40:28.480
Run, do anything.

00:40:28.480 --> 00:40:29.430
And thank you so much.

